WEBVTT

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:04.620
EMILY LISA: Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.350
We're excited today to talk
about agentic analytics

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.320
with Power BI and
Microsoft Fabric.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.710
My name is Emily Lisa, and I'm a
Principal Group Product Manager

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.240
at Microsoft.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.410
SUJATA NARAYANA: And
I'm Sujata Narayana.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.410
I'm also a Principal Group
Product Manager at Microsoft.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.080
And we're really excited to
get into some new innovation

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.160
and hopefully some things

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.650
that you guys will really
enjoy seeing here.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.070
EMILY LISA: Great.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.660
So we will start
off this session

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.560
with a quick introduction to the
concept of agentic analytics.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.070
Then we will go over in more
detail end-to-end agentic

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.950
analytics with Power BI
and Microsoft Fabric.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.420
Then I'll hand it off to Sujata,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.990
who will cover analytics
with Fabric apps.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.820
So to go ahead and get started,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.010
what really is
agentic development

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.380
for Power BI and
Fabric creators?

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.770
What this really means is it's a
way to use agentic experiences

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:57.420
with AI agents, skills, and
MCP servers to help do all

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.360
of those different creator
activities that you know

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.970
and love today, but with
the power of AI using LLMs.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.400
And so what we really
are looking to support

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.790
with agentic development with
Power BI is both greenfield

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.390
and brownfield scenarios.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.850
So these are just
simple examples,

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.040
but what we really mean by
greenfield is those creating

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.810
from scratch experiences,

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.200
such as creating a net new
semantic model, that you bring

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.670
in data, transform, do ETL on
top of it, shape the model,

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.860
and then build a
report afterwards.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.890
Similarly, an example of
a brownfield scenario is

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.990
when you're enhancing
something that already exists.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.660
So you might already have
your Power BI semantic model

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.090
or your Power BI report,
and there's some kind

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.790
of new requirement that comes
in from your leadership chain

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.880
or a customer, and you want to
adjust what you already have

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.910
to be in a shape that
meets new incoming changes

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.140
and requirements.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.420
So we can also help
those scenarios

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.400
with agentic
development as well.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.130
So we're really looking to
support both of these kind

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:00.660
of scenarios using AI to
help experts do even more,

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.610
even faster than they can today.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.600
And so let's take a
look at what we mean

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.560
by end-to-end agentic analytics.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.220
So, a few months ago, there
was a GitHub repository

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.320
that was shared called
Skills for Fabric.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.200
And what this is is it's
essentially a repository

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.770
that supports all sorts of
different agent skills for all

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.290
of your different Microsoft
Fabric scenarios for you

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.590
to be able to use
agents to do all sorts

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.750
of different Fabric
development tasks.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.950
So this spans across things like
lakehouses, notebooks, reports,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.240
semantic models, really the
whole Fabric ecosystem for you

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.830
to be more productive and
move faster using AI.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.530
And there's tons of flexibility
with using this repository.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.060
You could use it in all sorts

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.430
of different developer
environments, such as things

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.740
like GitHub Copilot
CLI or VS Code.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.980
And this really helps you move
even faster and allows you

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.160
to focus more on defining
the context and really the

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.080
"why" behind what you're
trying to create,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.990
rather than spending
hours and hours clicking

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.750
through every single step
to implement the solution.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.110
Really helps the LLM do some
of that heavy lifting work

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:15.480
and allows you to focus on those
high-level goals and outcomes.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.250
And so we have this link
here to try it out.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.010
I'd highly recommend trying out
the Fabric Skills repository

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.600
for all of your Fabric needs.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.390
But what we really want to focus
on here is how you can use this

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.910
for Power BI creation scenarios.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.030
Starting with our modeling
experiences, which we support

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.210
with the Power BI
modeling MCP server.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.310
Now, in the Fabric
Skills repository,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.250
there is a skill called
Power BI model authoring.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.300
And what this really does is
help you craft your semantic

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.470
models in a way using natural
language to really speed

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.510
up things that used
to take a long time

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.460
through clicks or scripting.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.510
Now you can do it easier

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.810
than ever using the
modeling MCP server.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.660
So the skill in the Fabric
Skills repository calls the

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.860
modeling MCP server and has
knowledge in the markdown files

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.700
to help you enhance your models
to follow best practices.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.800
And one additional
enhancement on top

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.770
of that is we now make
it easier than ever

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.290
to get started using
the modeling MCP server

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.300
from the web modeling view.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.730
All you have to do is click
on the "Copilot" button

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.290
in web model view, and this
will pop up a Copilot side chat

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.930
where you can use this to give
your natural language prompts

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.930
to make changes to your semantic
model, to make changes such as

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.420
to your DAX measures,
relationships,

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.110
your column properties, all of
those different rich semantics,

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.050
being able to modify them
using natural language.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.440
You can even get suggestions on
how to make your model better

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.980
for your reports and
optimize better for AI.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.060
So you can both use all these
really rich modeling experiences

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.650
in your developer environments,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:53.620
and even coming soon
in web modeling.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.760
Now, the next skill
that we support

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.000
in the Fabric Skills repository
is the much-anticipated report

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.410
authoring skill.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.540
We're really excited
to ship this live

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.950
for the first time at Build.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.860
And what this really does is
help you both create net new

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.910
reports as well as edit your
existing Power BI reports.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.770
What this does is you use the
PBIP file and desktop to be able

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.260
to create and edit your reports.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.390
And it really helps you a
lot of the heavy lifting

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:24.900
of all those operations that
traditionally take a long time

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:26.610
with doing draggy,
clicky, clicky,

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.650
draggy, droppy operations.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.950
Now you can use natural
language for all of them.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:35.010
Some key things that you can do
is update report themes based

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.260
on requirements, maybe
even a screenshot,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.500
as well as modify different
visuals and report elements

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.430
to meet all the requirements
that you have for your reports.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:44.760
All right.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.440
Well, enough talking
about these skills.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.940
Let's see what it
looks like actually

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.160
in action with some demos.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.650
So, in the first demo that I
want to show, this is going

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.210
to be a greenfield scenario.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.210
So in this case, I'm creating
a net new semantic model

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.240
and report completely from
scratch with the assistance

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.330
of AI using the Fabric
Skills repository.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.040
So here in my setup, I have

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.190
on the left-hand side
an empty PBIP file open

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.720
in Power BI Desktop.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:10.800
And on the right-hand side,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.260
I have GitHub Copilot
CLI connected

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.570
to this instance of
Power BI Desktop.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.420
Now, to create a net new
semantic model within this file,

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.820
all I have to do is give a
really simple prompt, such as,

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.600
"Create a new semantic model
using data at ISQL database,"

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.740
and it'll just go ahead

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.320
and create this new
semantic model for me.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.280
I'm able to see all the changes
validated on Power BI Desktop

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.760
on the left-hand side, while
the LLM is also giving updates

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.130
of all the changes it's making
on the right-hand side,

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.170
including a helpful summary.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.360
Now I'm able to see I have
a beautiful star schema,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.670
but I want to take
this one step further.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.880
My organization defines our
semantic modeling best practices

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.350
in a shared markdown file.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.740
So I just give this file as
context to the LLM and ask it

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.080
to update my semantic
model to follow all

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.870
of those well-known best
practices, which it goes ahead

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.820
and does for me, giving
helpful summaries.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.680
And I even see a
description added

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.710
to my column matching
the best practices.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.870
Now, as a next step, I want
to create a report off

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.250
of this semantic model.

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:09.870
What I do is I define each

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:12.030
of the report pages the
requirements I have

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.860
for that page of what
I want it to look

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.160
like within these two
different markdown files.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.390
And then I go ahead and give it
as context to the LLM and say,

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:24.060
"Go and make this
report for me."

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:24.990
And then what I'm able

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.650
to see afterwards is it creates
this beautiful new report,

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:30.930
all based off of my written --

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.630
the requirements within
my markdown files.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:36.990
Now I want to take this one step
further and style this report

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.580
to match theming and branding

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.100
that I desire for
my organization.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.430
So I give it a reference
image of a sample report page

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.560
that I want the rest of
my report to look like,

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.220
as well as my company's logo,
for it to follow as theming

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.350
and styling best practices.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.200
All it took was a really simple
prompt and these two pieces

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.020
of information as context,
and it was able to modify

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.020
that entire report for me
to match all the styling

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.010
and theming guidance that I
really wanted for this report.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:07.740
All of these tasks, as many of
you Power BI creators know,

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.420
often take many hours to do
fine-tuning and clicking

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:13.560
and moving things around.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.280
Now it's easier than ever,
thanks to the help of AI

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.230
and the Fabric
Skills repository,

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:22.620
making it so I could focus on
defining the what of what I want

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.870
for my data solution,
rather than going

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:29.730
through all the manual processes
of making those things happen.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.940
So that was a greenfield
scenario, creating completely

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:37.140
from scratch using AI, a net
new semantic model and report.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.090
But as we talked about earlier,

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:41.000
it's not just greenfield
scenarios we support.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.700
We also support brownfield
scenarios of being able

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.160
to edit existing things
that you already have.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.680
We know many of you already
have existing semantic models

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.510
and reports that you
either want to fine-tune

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.450
for new requirements or
just want to modify based

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.860
on any new preferences you have.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:58.230
Let's take a look at what a
brownfield scenario looks

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.260
like for a report.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.470
So in this case, I want to level
up one of my existing reports

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:08.580
in one prompt using the same
Fabric Skills repository.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:09.900
Here, I have my report page,

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:13.350
which maybe could be a bit
more visually appealing.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.380
It's not following some of
the reporting best practices.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.410
I want to make it better
and more executive-ready.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:22.230
So all I do is I ask Copilot

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.150
to help improve my
Adventure Works report.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:25.950
I want to have a
more modern feel,

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:28.380
have a clean editorial
landing page

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.390
with intuitive page navigation,

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:33.360
and really look more polished
and executive-ready.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.990
In Plan mode in GitHub Copilot
CLI, I'm given different options

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:38.760
of what this page can look like.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:41.940
I review these and I go ahead
and go with one of the options

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:43.500
that was suggested to me.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.960
And then the LLM goes ahead
and makes the changes

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.930
to my report based on that
plan, and it looks beautiful

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.060
with consistent
styling and theme.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.300
I had that really nice landing
page at the beginning.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.030
This is in a much better state,
where I'm ready to share it

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.850
with my executives
and my larger team.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.760
And it was so easy to get
started, all in one prompt,

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.610
thanks to the assistance of
the Fabric Skills repository

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:09.920
with the Power BI report
authoring skill within there.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:13.430
So that was really it for the
end-to-end agentic analytics

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:14.900
part of the presentation.

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.390
Now I'm going to hand
it off to Sujata

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:20.310
to cover the next section.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:20.670
SUJATA NARAYANA: All right.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:21.990
Thank you, Emily, so much

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.300
for showing us all the
innovation that's happening

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.360
around agentic development
in Power BI reports

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.400
and Power BI semantic models.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.340
Now let's switch gears here
a little bit, because I want

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:34.950
to talk about something
that is brand new, in fact,

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.170
released just at this
conference, which,

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:38.880
let's look at Fabric apps.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:40.350
And specifically, I want to talk

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:44.130
about analytics use cases
within Fabric apps.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:47.460
So AI is really democratizing
a lot that's happening

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:48.960
with web app development.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.750
We're seeing all kinds
of scenarios where MVPs

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:55.710
like Injae Park, Reid Havens are
building end-to-end web apps

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:57.870
for enterprise analytics.

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:00.750
And because AI is making it
so much easier to do this,

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:03.420
we're even seeing citizen
developers being able

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.780
to achieve so much more here.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:11.490
Now, where custom web apps
add value beyond reports is

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.950
that developers that
need more flexibility

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:17.400
or have higher custom
requirements can really take

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:20.190
advantage of going the
custom web app route.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:23.670
Because with a custom web app,
you're getting full custom UX.

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:26.070
So if you want things
like responsive layouts,

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:30.750
unlimited UI controls, or even
full-blown multi-step workflows,

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:32.970
you can get that with
a custom web app.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.590
And also, if you have scenarios

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:37.770
where you want fully
customizable actions,

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.230
these web apps can be really
helpful for that as well,

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:42.870
because you're getting a much
more seamless integration

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:44.820
for building things
like decision loops,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.000
supporting custom code
and custom integrations,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.140
because at the end of the day,

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:53.130
custom web apps
are all just code.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:54.780
And now, here's the caveat.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.390
Even though AI is
democratizing a lot

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.840
of web app development today,
the reality is that a lot

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:04.020
of these vibe-coded apps
never reach production.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:08.010
So majority of these apps, let's
say if you built on Lovable,

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:11.820
Streamlit, or other surface
areas, may not reach production

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:15.090
because they may miss things
like enterprise-grade security,

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:19.290
governance, reliability, and
more operational controls.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:22.560
And so even though AI helps
you get 80% of the way,

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:26.370
that additional 20% of making
it ready for production,

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.460
if it's not there, it's
just not enough to have

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:32.430
to your customers
or your end users.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:34.770
And so this is something
that we want to tackle

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:36.960
with Microsoft Fabric.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:39.660
And that's now
introducing Fabric apps.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.640
So you may have seen in
some of the other sessions,

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:44.280
we made a huge announcement
around Fabric apps,

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:46.950
which basically what that
is, is a Fabric backend

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:49.530
for building
enterprise-grade apps.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:52.680
Now, let's talk about this
a little bit more detail.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.830
So what this is, is Rayfin
is the project name,

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.860
which is allows for a
CLI-based experience.

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:00.600
So not only is this
developer-friendly,

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.670
but it's very AI-friendly too.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.090
So here, you see an example
from our NPM package,

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.600
which you can connect to your
CLI experiences in order

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.660
to have your coding
agent of choice be able

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:16.650
to build end-to-end apps that
are all backed in Fabric.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:20.850
Now, what does this relate to
Power BI or semantic models?

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.120
Well, to start with, is
Fabric apps can be built

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:25.230
on semantic models.

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:28.140
So that same foundation
that you're getting

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:30.900
to build your reports
from your analyst,

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:35.490
your developers can now build
custom web apps on that as well.

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.340
And so what this
unlocks is really good

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:40.710
for enterprise analytics.

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:42.120
So not only are you able

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:45.210
to build entirely
blown custom web apps,

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:48.750
but these web apps are
live -- using live data.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.820
They are using
optimized queries.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:54.120
And, of course, we are using
visuals that are very consistent

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.060
and meet enterprise-grade
requirements while

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.590
being AI-first.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:02.610
And by being secure connection
to your Fabric data,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:05.460
and also the enterprise scale
and governance that you expect,

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:07.140
you can more safely deploy this

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:11.050
to your enterprise
customers and end users.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:17.040
So let's talk a bit about how
to get started with this.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.340
So Fabric apps includes
a data app template.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:23.580
So, to start with, is you can
either choose the template

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:25.350
within the CLI experience.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:28.110
So when you do your
NPM create command,

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:31.380
you'll need to add a statement
that says template data app.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:34.980
Or if you're in Fabric, you can
also initiate the creation of it

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:36.150
through Fabric as well.

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:38.760
So you can pick the
template called Data App.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:39.690
And let's talk a little bit

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.760
of what's happening
behind the scenes.

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:44.370
So the data app template
is really comprised

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:46.140
of three key things.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.240
First is a data package
that allows you

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:51.120
to securely connect
to your Fabric data.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:52.680
And next is a visual package,

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:57.420
which is using a Vega-Lite
component and React components

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:59.610
so that you're getting
visualizations

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:02.580
that are really optimized
for AI, make it really great

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:06.390
for building a ton of
different flexible visuals,

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:07.830
while also staying true

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:10.680
to certain
enterprise-grade defaults.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.740
And then, of course, we
have a set of agent skills

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.520
that are helping teach the LLM
how to properly query the data,

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:21.810
how to handle Fabric formats,
write Vega-Lite visual specs,

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:24.510
and, of course, apply
design best practices

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:27.810
so that these applications
look really polished

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:29.160
and professional.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:32.670
And all of these templates
are built directly on Rayfin,

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:35.700
so directly on Fabric apps,
so that you can leverage it

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:39.210
with your Rayfin experiences.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:43.260
Now, let's take a look at a
demo of what this looks like.

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:47.040
So let's see a scenario where
I'm going to use a few prompts

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:51.120
and an MD file to generate
a production-ready app.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:54.270
So starting with my semantic
model, you can see here,

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:56.130
it's using a lot of
different tables,

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:57.660
a lot of different data here.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.830
I want to take this data that is
in the form of a semantic model.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:03.450
I want to build an app from it.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.060
So, to start with,
is I'll have a spec

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:09.930
that actually AI helped me
create around my requirements.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:11.670
And even though AI
helped me create it,

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:13.830
I can always go
manually tweak this.

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:17.010
So here's an example where
I want to add a hover card.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:19.530
So let's say I want
a image, an email.

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:22.650
I can go add those hover
card tooltips there.

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:25.440
And the next part, I'm going
to go scaffold that project.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.480
So I use that NPM
create command,

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:31.140
and I make sure I specify
the template of data app.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:35.610
So as you see here, the CLI
experience is creating --

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.320
scaffolding the project for me,

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:41.070
and now I have the files
all available on my local.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:43.050
And so once I have
this available,

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:45.180
I'm going to navigate
to that file.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:49.080
So if I go here, I navigate
to my project file,

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:50.760
and then I'm going to
go and publish this

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:52.290
to my Fabric workspace.

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:54.420
So, at this point, I've
just provisioned the app.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.930
I haven't yet created the
exact app idea I have.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:16:59.700
And this is where the
fun part happens.

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:02.730
So, at this point, I'm going
to give my coding agent

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:08.250
of choice a link to my spec file
and a link to my semantic model.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.980
So once it has these
two critical details,

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:14.970
I ask it to go build the app
and deploy it when it's ready.

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:17.460
So this takes roughly
about 20 minutes or so

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:20.460
of your coding agent
processing this request.

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:24.510
And when it's done, you get a
full-blown production-ready

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:27.870
application that matches the
requirements that you put

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:30.720
out in your spec. And
so here, for example,

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:34.830
I have a three-page application
here that includes things

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:39.060
like filtering, tooltips, like
I mentioned, that hover card

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:42.870
that appears, even having
more customization

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:45.780
like adding a
full-blown dark mode.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.660
And so it's really quite
powerful of the type

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.960
of data application that
you can create here.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:56.100
Now, let's say you want to
customize this even further.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.500
And that's the beauty
with working with AI,

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:02.130
is you really just need
to update that MD spec.

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:03.780
So let's see an example of this.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:06.000
So let's say I want to add
a little bit more features

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:07.260
to this application.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:08.940
Let's say I want a landing page,

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:11.820
I want persona-specific
selection,

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:13.860
and I even want a calendar tab.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.200
So I can go add all
these requirements

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.360
to the spec and update it.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:20.640
And then I just hand
Copilot that updated spec.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:24.500
So I just say, "Hey, update the
app using the latest spec."

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:28.160
And as it's going to go process
through that request again --

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:31.040
again, takes about
20 minutes or so,

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:35.330
and you have your updated
application right here.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:39.240
So you see the
persona-specific selection.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.660
So you have different set
of pages for each persona.

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:45.840
So the executive page is a
little bit more focused.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:47.190
And then, of course, you have

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:49.350
that calendar view
like I mentioned.

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:55.200
So this is really bringing in a
lot more of actionable elements

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:58.740
to this application because I
can now track the calendars

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.980
for the different collaborations
I have coming up.

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:04.710
And I can even go and add
those calendar events

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:07.560
to my Outlook calendar itself.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.140
So you're really kind of
combining the elements

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:12.990
of both the analytics
with also the elements

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:15.750
of the action-orientated apps.

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:18.810
So it's really about
bringing your data alive

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:22.140
and really customizing
your app however you want,

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:24.090
and doing so in a
prompt-based way.

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:27.180
So leveraging AI with
what it's best for,

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:30.480
especially the coding agents,
to really generate that code

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:32.700
from you, completely
from scratch.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.890
And so this is really
showing the value

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:39.450
of what the data app template
can offer as you are trying

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:43.340
out the new Rayfin and
Fabric apps experience.

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:46.820
So now let's recap what you
get with data apps in Fabric.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:49.070
So, to start off with,
is that connection

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:51.860
to semantic models
is so critical.

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:53.120
Right out of the
box, you're going

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.810
to get a secure authentication
to your semantic models,

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:59.870
which I'm sure many of you
have tons of semantic models

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:02.990
where you're eager to try
this new experience with.

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:04.970
Next, you're going
to get the AI-driven

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.560
analytics capabilities.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:10.190
So out of the box, being able
to have AI understand how

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:11.820
to write DAX queries,

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:13.680
making sure you're
getting optimized queries

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:17.190
so that you're visualizing the
right data, the right insights,

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.160
and, of course, formulating
that really well in visuals.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:23.400
And then speaking of our
visuals, like I mentioned,

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:28.350
these visuals use the Vega-Lite
visualization library,

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:32.310
which make it really easy
to build AI-first visuals

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:34.590
and using enterprise-grade
defaults.

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:37.770
And so we've added additional
customizations on top of that

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:40.770
to make sure there's
consistency in tooltips,

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:42.240
make sure there's accessibility,

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:45.090
and all the enterprise-grade
requirements

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:46.590
that you would expect.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:50.010
And more coming soon is that
we want to add more sources.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:53.760
So we know that there's a lot
of other sources in Fabric

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.560
where you may not want
to connect directly

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:57.450
to a semantic model, but you
may want to connect directly

00:20:57.450 --> 00:21:01.080
to other sources, let's
say, a SQL DB directly,

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.810
or maybe a lakehouse or
a warehouse directly.

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:07.050
So we do plan to add more
sources coming soon.

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:10.440
We also want to have more
out-of-the-box signal detection.

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:14.490
Of course, because web apps are
just code, you can always prompt

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:17.340
that yourself, but we want to
make it way easier for you

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:21.180
to get that signal detection
right out of the box.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.240
And same goes for
custom actions as well.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:27.390
So integration with things
like user data functions,

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:30.180
integration with things
like operational agents.

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.550
We want to enable a little bit
more streamlined integration

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:35.790
with any sort of custom actions
or custom agents that you want

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:37.650
to bring into the picture.

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:39.510
And last but not least,
we want to make sure

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:41.910
that you also support
embedded scenarios.

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:45.150
So in the first release -- right
now, the data app is limited

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:48.000
to just being hosted within
Fabric, and you see it

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:49.890
within the Fabric portal.

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:53.040
But we want to also make sure
that in the future releases,

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:55.590
you can be able to embed
this in your own application

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:58.260
or embed it in other areas.

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:01.950
So with that, we hope this
gave you a good insight

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:04.650
into what we're doing
in terms of innovation

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:06.930
for our analytics experiences,

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:10.140
both across Power BI
and Microsoft Fabric.

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:13.200
And for those that are
interested in learning more

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.980
about any of the things you've
seen here check out our blog

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:21.150
at aka.ms/Azure-Data-Build26,

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:23.370
and also explore
our documentation

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:26.190
and our community
site to learn more

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:28.320
and engage with the community.

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:29.010
Thank you so much.

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:32.460
We hope you enjoyed this
presentation and looking forward

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:35.860
to your feedback on all
this new innovation.
